IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- coa-core.l4: the shared vocabulary for the Companies Act 2006 (United Kingdom), 2006 c. 46.
-- DECLARE and shared constants only; no rules, no #EVAL, no #ASSERT.
--
-- READ-ONLY for every part agent. A part that needs a type or constructor this module lacks defines it in its
-- own module under its own section prefix, records it in NEEDS-CORE.md, and the lead hoists it at the merge.
--
-- SOURCE. The text is a plain-text rendering of the Act from legislation.gov.uk (Open Government Licence v3.0)
-- made by a converter script (see inputs/SOURCES.md). It is not the official text, and nothing here is
-- "verified against the official text". Quote lines below are checked against that rendering by srcrefs.py.
--
-- TARGET. The stable `l4` (main): 1.6.110 (main, e51181d, 2026-11-11); also read by main 1.6.109. No section GIVEN,
-- no REFUSE, no sets, no TYPICALLY, no opaque DECLARE, no YMD: dates are written `Date day month year`.
--
-- MEMBERSHIP TEST for this module: could a witness of ordinary competence testify to the fact, or does answering
-- it require reading the Act? Only the first kind is here. Whether a company IS "small", "quoted", "traded",
-- "a subsidiary" or "dormant" is a rule, and belongs with the section that says so (coa-definitions.l4 for ss 1-6
-- and 1158-1174; the part that owns the section otherwise).
--
-- MONEY is NUMBER, in pounds sterling unless the section names another currency. A PERCENTAGE is a NUMBER
-- fraction (0.25 is 25%). A DATE is `Date day month year`.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHERE a company is registered, and how its members' liability is limited
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The three registration jurisdictions the Act distinguishes: "Great Britain" in s 4(3) is the first two together.
DECLARE `Place of registration` IS ONE OF
    `England and Wales`
    `Scotland`
    `Northern Ireland`

-- src:29 | 3 Limited and unlimited companies
-- src:30 | (1) A company is a “limited company” if the liability of its members is limited by its constitution.
-- src:31 | It may be limited by shares or limited by guarantee.
-- src:32 | (2) If their liability is limited to the amount, if any, unpaid on the shares held by them, the company is “limited by shares”.
-- src:33 | (3) If their liability is limited to such amount as the members undertake to contribute to the assets of the company in the event of its being wound up, the company is “limited by guarantee”.
-- src:34 | (4) If there is no limit on the liability of its members, the company is an “unlimited company”.
@ref s 3 Limited and unlimited companies (src:29-34)
DECLARE `Liability of members` IS ONE OF
    `liability not limited`
    `liability limited to the amount, if any, unpaid on the shares held`
    `liability limited to the amount the members undertake to contribute on a winding up`
    `liability limited in some other way by the constitution`

-- src:36 | (1) A “private company” is any company that is not a public company.
-- src:37 | (2) A “public company” is a company limited by shares or limited by guarantee and having a share capital—
-- src:38 | (a) whose certificate of incorporation states that it is a public company, and
-- src:39 | (b) in relation to which the requirements of this Act, or the former Companies Acts, as to registration or re-registration as a public company have been complied with on or after the relevant date.
@ref s 4(1)-(2) Private and public companies (src:36-39)
DECLARE `Company profile` HAS
    `place of registration` IS A `Place of registration`
    `liability of the members` IS A `Liability of members`
    `a share capital` IS A BOOLEAN
    `certificate of incorporation stating that it is a public company` IS A BOOLEAN

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHO: the kinds of person, the individual in the seat, and the parties a rule can name
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:16601 | “body corporate” and “corporation” include a body incorporated outside the United Kingdom, but do not include—
-- src:16602 | (a) a corporation sole, or
-- src:16603 | (b) a partnership that, whether or not a legal person, is not regarded as a body corporate under the law by which it is governed;
-- src:16609 | “firm” means any entity, whether or not a legal person, that is not an individual and includes a body corporate, a corporation sole and a partnership or other unincorporated association;
@ref s 1173(1) "body corporate", "corporation" and "firm" (src:16601-16603 and src:16609)
DECLARE `Kind of person` IS ONE OF
    `an individual`
    `a company registered under the Companies Act 2006`
    `another body corporate incorporated in the United Kingdom`
    `a body corporate incorporated outside the United Kingdom`
    `a corporation sole`
    `a partnership`
    `an unincorporated association`

-- The seat: an individual who is, or is about to become, a director, a company secretary or a member of a company,
-- or who deals with one. Identity is the reference field: compare it, never the whole record.
DECLARE Individual HAS
    `reference for the individual` IS A STRING
    `date of birth` IS A DATE

-- Every party that a deontic rule or a jural relation in this Act can name. Officials and courts are here as the
-- parties the text names; the seat is still the individual (no seat for an official).
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    Company
    Director
    `Shadow director`
    `Company secretary`
    Member
    `Subscriber to the memorandum`
    `Officer in default`
    Auditor
    Creditor
    `Debenture holder`
    `Person with significant control`
    Registrar
    `Secretary of State`
    Court
    `Takeover Panel`
    `Competent authority`

-- A generic action, so that a part can write `DEONTIC Party Action of a party` without declaring an action type.
-- The first actor-typed field is the performer. A part that needs a typed action declares it under its own prefix.
DECLARE `Action of a party` HAS
    performer IS A Party
    recipient IS A Party
    `what is done` IS A STRING

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- DECISIONS and DOCUMENTS the Act keeps returning to
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:3732 | 282 Ordinary resolutions
-- src:3733 | (1) An ordinary resolution of the members (or of a class of members) of a company means a resolution that is passed by a simple majority.
-- src:3734 | (2) A written resolution is passed by a simple majority if it is passed by members representing a simple majority of the total voting rights of eligible members (see Chapter 2).
-- src:3735 | (3) A resolution passed at a meeting on a show of hands is passed by a simple majority if it is passed by a simple majority of the votes cast by those entitled to vote
-- src:3736 | (4) A resolution passed on a poll taken at a meeting is passed by a simple majority if it is passed by members representing a simple majority of the total voting rights of members who (being entitled to do so) vote in person, by proxy or in advance (see section 322A) on the resolution.
-- src:3737 | (5) Anything that may be done by ordinary resolution may also be done by special resolution.
-- src:3738 | 283 Special resolutions
-- src:3739 | (1) A special resolution of the members (or of a class of members) of a company means a resolution passed by a majority of not less than 75%.
-- src:3740 | (2) A written resolution is passed by a majority of not less than 75% if it is passed by members representing not less than 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members (see Chapter 2).
-- src:3741 | (3) Where a resolution of a private company is passed as a written resolution—
-- src:3742 | (a) the resolution is not a special resolution unless it stated that it was proposed as a special resolution, and
-- src:3743 | (b) if the resolution so stated, it may only be passed as a special resolution.
-- src:3744 | (4) A resolution passed at a meeting on a show of hands is passed by a majority of not less than 75% if it is passed by not less than 75% of the votes cast by those entitled to vote.
-- src:3745 | (5) A resolution passed on a poll taken at a meeting is passed by a majority of not less than 75% if it is passed by members representing not less than 75% of the total voting rights of the members who (being entitled to do so) vote in person, by proxy or in advance (see section 322A) on the resolution.
-- src:3746 | (6) Where a resolution is passed at a meeting—
-- src:3747 | (a) the resolution is not a special resolution unless the notice of the meeting included the text of the resolution and specified the intention to propose the resolution as a special resolution, and
-- src:3748 | (b) if the notice of the meeting so specified, the resolution may only be passed as a special resolution.
@ref ss 282-283 Ordinary and special resolutions (src:3732-3748)
DECLARE `Kind of resolution` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary resolution`
    `a special resolution`

DECLARE `Way a resolution is passed` IS ONE OF
    `as a written resolution`
    `at a meeting on a show of hands`
    `at a meeting on a poll`

DECLARE `Kind of meeting` IS ONE OF
    `a general meeting`
    `an annual general meeting`
    `a class meeting`
    `a meeting of the directors`

-- src:16546 | 1168 Hard copy and electronic form and related expressions
-- src:16547 | (1) The following provisions apply for the purposes of the Companies Acts.
-- src:16548 | (2) A document or information is sent or supplied in hard copy form if it is sent or supplied in a paper copy or similar form capable of being read.
-- src:16549 | References to hard copy have a corresponding meaning.
-- src:16550 | (3) A document or information is sent or supplied in electronic form if it is sent or supplied—
-- src:16551 | (a) by electronic means (for example, by e-mail or fax), or
-- src:16552 | (b) by any other means while in an electronic form (for example, sending a disk by post).
-- src:16553 | References to electronic copy have a corresponding meaning.
-- src:16554 | (4) A document or information is sent or supplied by electronic means if it is—
-- src:16555 | (a) sent initially and received at its destination by means of electronic equipment for the processing (which expression includes digital compression) or storage of data, and
-- src:16556 | (b) entirely transmitted, conveyed and received by wire, by radio, by optical means or by other electromagnetic means.
-- src:16557 | References to electronic means have a corresponding meaning.
@ref s 1168(2)-(4) Hard copy and electronic form (src:16546-16557)
DECLARE `Form of document` IS ONE OF
    `hard copy form`
    `electronic form`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TIME: periods, and the date of enactment
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Constructors are capitalised so that a part's own `GIVEN days` or `GIVEN months` never collides with them.
DECLARE `Unit of time` IS ONE OF
    Days
    `Working days`
    Weeks
    Months
    Years

DECLARE Period HAS
    `number of units` IS A NUMBER
    unit IS A `Unit of time`

-- src:4 | [8th November 2006]
@ref the date of enactment (src:4)
`the Companies Act 2006 was enacted on` MEANS Date 8 11 2006

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- OFFENCES and PENALTIES: the vocabulary the Act repeats in nearly every Part
-- (measured on the text: "standard scale" 174 times, "daily default fine" 140, "on summary conviction" 267)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 1128 and the offence sections distinguish where a person is convicted.
DECLARE `Mode of conviction` IS ONE OF
    `on conviction on indictment`
    `on summary conviction`
    `on summary conviction in England and Wales`
    `on summary conviction in Scotland`
    `on summary conviction in Northern Ireland`

-- "a fine", "a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum", "level 3 on the standard scale", "one-tenth of level 3",
-- "one-fifth of the statutory maximum". A fraction of 1 means the amount itself. The pounds a level or the
-- statutory maximum stands for are set outside the Act (INPUTS, never guessed).
DECLARE `Fine limit` IS ONE OF
    `a fine without a stated limit`
    `a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum` HAS `fraction of the statutory maximum` IS A NUMBER
    `a fine not exceeding a level on the standard scale` HAS level IS A NUMBER, `fraction of the level` IS A NUMBER
    `a fine not exceeding an amount in pounds` HAS pounds IS A NUMBER

-- src:16089 | 1125 Meaning of “daily default fine”
-- src:16090 | (1) This section defines what is meant in the Companies Acts where it is provided that a person guilty of an offence is liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding a specified amount “and, for continued contravention, a daily default fine” not exceeding a specified amount.
-- src:16091 | (2) This means that the person is liable on a second or subsequent summary conviction of the offence to a fine not exceeding the latter amount for each day on which the contravention is continued (instead of being liable to a fine not exceeding the former amount).
@ref s 1125 Meaning of "daily default fine" (src:16089-16091)
DECLARE Penalty HAS
    `how convicted` IS A `Mode of conviction`
    `imprisonment not exceeding` IS A MAYBE Period
    `fine not exceeding` IS A MAYBE `Fine limit`
    `daily default fine for continued contravention` IS A MAYBE `Fine limit`
    `imprisonment and fine together` IS A BOOLEAN
